Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/2] wifi: brcmfmac: recover firmware-rejected GTK replacement

This RFC proposes a narrowly scoped workaround for GTK replacement
failures observed on a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W, BCM43430/2, with firmware
9.88.4.77 (FWID 01-3b307371). Ordinary group-key rotation can disconnect
the station when firmware rejects replacement of an occupied slot with
BCME_REPLAY. Clearing that slot and retrying once recovered the observed
failures in bounded tests on the distribution kernel.

Patch 1 adds per-call firmware status without changing legacy callers.
Patch 2 uses it to gate recovery on the exact firmware error, chip,
revision, firmware build, key type, old slot contents and a changed key.
The series excludes the experimental trace instrumentation.

Review is requested on the firmware-specific scope and on whether
clearing the occupied slot is an acceptable workaround. The host cache
cannot establish the firmware's complete replay state. Hardware replay
resistance testing and maintainer/vendor review are still outstanding.
No over-the-air replay attack has been demonstrated by the investigation.

The recorded hardware tests used 6.18.39+rpt-rpi-v8 and the equivalent
scoped backport. Four ten-minute rotations completed, including two
recoveries, without reassociation before planned rollback. Broadcast and
multicast traffic was observed after every rotation. Ping received
2392/2400 replies across the capture; those eight losses cannot be placed
relative to the recovery events. A stock-driver retest failed again.
This mainline port has not been run on hardware.

Reproduction requires the affected firmware in station mode on a WPA2
CCMP network with periodic GTK rotation. Associate, then observe enough
rotations to replace both occupied group-key slots. The failing request
uses changed key material and a valid receive sequence; the captured
rekey EAPOL counters advance. The initially used slot failed on reuse in
the observed setup. A different access point may not reproduce it.

The base is pinned mainline commit 893e11787f78, rather than a claim of
runtime validation on mainline or wireless-next. No introducing Linux
commit has been established, so no speculative Fixes tag or stable
backport request is included. See upstream/README.md in the preparation
repository for reproducible checks and remaining submission requirements.

AI assistance was used for investigation, coding, tests and this draft.
The patches contain Assisted-by: LLM. This is an unsigned preparation
artifact awaiting human review and DCO certification; it has not been
submitted to any mailing list.

